.SH NAME
Dokumentacja katalogu C:/PinterestApp/WebPhotoPage/Frontend/node_modules/set-cookie-parser
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Opis szczegółowy"
.PP 

.SH "set-cookie-parser"
.PP
\fR\fP \fR\fP \fR\fP

.PP
.PP

.PP
ℹ️ \fBNote for current users:\fP I'm considering some changes for the next major version and would appreciate your feedback: https://github.com/nfriedly/set-cookie-parser/discussions/68

.PP
.PP

.PP
Parses set-cookie headers into JavaScript objects

.PP
Accepts a single \fRset-cookie\fP header value, an array of \fRset-cookie\fP header values, a Node\&.js response object, or a \fRfetch()\fP \fRResponse\fP object that may have 0 or more \fRset-cookie\fP headers\&.

.PP
Also accepts an optional options object\&. Defaults:

.PP
.PP
.nf
{
    decodeValues: true,  // Calls decodeURIComponent on each value \- default: true
    map: false,          // Return an object instead of an array \- default: false
    silent: false,       // Suppress the warning that is logged when called on a request instead of a response \- default: false
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Returns either an array of cookie objects or a map of name => cookie object with \fR{map: true}\fP\&. Each cookie object will have, at a minimum \fRname\fP and \fRvalue\fP properties, and may have additional properties depending on the set-cookie header:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRname\fP - cookie name (string)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRvalue\fP - cookie value (string)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpath\fP - URL path to limit the scope to (string or undefined)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRdomain\fP - domain to expand the scope to (string or undefined, may begin with "\&." to indicate the named domain or any subdomain of it)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRexpires\fP - absolute expiration date for the cookie (Date object or undefined)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRmaxAge\fP - relative expiration time of the cookie in seconds from when the client receives it (integer or undefined)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Note: when using with \fRexpress's res\&.cookie() method\fP, multiply \fRmaxAge\fP by 1000 to convert to milliseconds\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsecure\fP - indicates cookie should only be sent over HTTPs (true or undefined)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRhttpOnly\fP - indicates cookie should \fInot\fP be accessible to client-side JavaScript (true or undefined)
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRsameSite\fP - indicates if cookie should be included in cross-site requests (\fRmore info\fP) (string or undefined)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
Note: valid values are \fR"Strict"\fP, \fR"Lax"\fP, and \fR"None"\fP, but set-cookie-parser coppies the value verbatim and does \fInot\fP perform any validation\&.
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRpartitioned\fP - indicates cookie should be scoped to the combination of 3rd party domain + top page domain (\fRmore info\fP) (true or undefined)
.PP

.PP
(The output format is loosely based on the input format of https://www.npmjs.com/package/cookie)
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install \-\-save set\-cookie\-parser
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.SS "Get array of cookie objects"
.PP
.nf
var http = require('http');
var setCookie = require('set\-cookie\-parser');

http\&.get('http://example\&.com', function(res) {
  var cookies = setCookie\&.parse(res, {
    decodeValues: true  // default: true
  });

  cookies\&.forEach(console\&.log);
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
Example output:

.PP
.PP
.nf
[
    {
        name: 'bam',
        value: 'baz'
    },
    {
        name: 'foo',
        value: 'bar',
        path: '/',
        expires: new Date('Tue Jul 01 2025 06:01:11 GMT\-0400 (EDT)'),
        maxAge: 1000,
        domain: '\&.example\&.com',
        secure: true,
        httpOnly: true,
        sameSite: 'lax'
    }
]
.fi
.PP
.SS "Get map of cookie objects"
.PP
.nf
var http = require('http');
var setCookie = require('set\-cookie\-parser');

http\&.get('http://example\&.com', function(res) {
  var cookies = setCookie\&.parse(res, {
    decodeValues: true,  // default: true
    map: true            // default: false
  });

  var desiredCookie = cookies['session'];
  console\&.log(desiredCookie);
});
.fi
.PP
 Example output: 
.PP
.nf
{
    bam: {
        name: 'bam',
        value: 'baz'
    },
    foo: {
        name: 'foo',
        value: 'bar',
        path: '/',
        expires: new Date('Tue Jul 01 2025 06:01:11 GMT\-0400 (EDT)'),
        maxAge: 1000,
        domain: '\&.example\&.com',
        secure: true,
        httpOnly: true,
        sameSite: 'lax'
    }
}

.fi
.PP
.SS "Creating a new, modified set-cookie header"
This library can be used in conjunction with the \fRcookie\fP library to modify and replace set-cookie headers:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const libCookie = require('cookie');
const setCookie = require('set\-cookie\-parser');

function modifySetCookie(res){
  // parse the set\-cookie headers with this library
  let cookies = setCookie\&.parse(res);
  
  // modify the cookies here
  // \&.\&.\&.
  
  // create new set\-cookie headers using the cookie library
  res\&.headers['set\-cookie'] = cookies\&.map(function(cookie) {
      return libCookie\&.serialize(cookie\&.name, cookie\&.value, cookie);
  });
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
See a real-world example of this in \fRunblocker\fP
.SH "Usage in React Native (and with some other fetch implementations)"
.PP
React Native follows the Fetch spec more closely and combines all of the Set-Cookie header values into a single string\&. The \fRsplitCookiesString\fP method reverses this\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
var setCookie = require('set\-cookie\-parser');

var response = fetch(/*\&.\&.\&.*/);

// This is mainly for React Native; Node\&.js does not combine set\-cookie headers\&.
var combinedCookieHeader = response\&.headers\&.get('Set\-Cookie');
var splitCookieHeaders = setCookie\&.splitCookiesString(combinedCookieHeader)
var cookies = setCookie\&.parse(splitCookieHeaders);

console\&.log(cookies); // should be an array of cookies
.fi
.PP

.PP
This behavior may become a default part of parse in the next major release, but requires the extra step for now\&.

.PP
Note that the \fRfetch()\fP spec now includes a \fRgetSetCookie()\fP method that provides un-combined \fRSet-Cookie\fP headers\&. This library will automatically use that method if it is present\&.
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "parse(input, [options])"
Parses cookies from a string, array of strings, or a http response object\&. Always returns an array, regardless of input format\&. (Unless the \fRmap\fP option is set, in which case it always returns an object\&.)
.SS "parseString(individualSetCookieHeader, [options])"
Parses a single set-cookie header value string\&. Options default is \fR{decodeValues: true}\fP\&. Used under-the-hood by \fRparse()\fP\&. Returns an object\&.
.SS "splitCookiesString(combinedSetCookieHeader)"
It's uncommon, but the HTTP spec does allow for multiple of the same header to have their values combined (comma-separated) into a single header\&. This method splits apart a combined header without choking on commas that appear within a cookie's value (or expiration date)\&. Returns an array of strings that may be passed to \fRparse()\fP\&.
